<!--
    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) Rikarin
    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

    Doc 36 § F7 wave 7. Doc 34's first file, and the one that had no panel: the names a shape may
    have, the tags it may carry, the body plans that require them, and what is wrong with the lot.
    The ledger sized it M in the seven-panel block with "port the readouts, keep the field rows
    (shape 2)", and unlike `FontView` — where that instruction turned out to be stale — it is exactly
    right here, for a reason that is not shape 2 at all.

    ⚠ **The field pane stays C# because it is a `switch` over the *type* of the selection, which is
    wave 4's trap with the sharpest edge it has.** `Restate` chooses between four shapes of pane by
    pattern-matching `selected`, and every readout inside the chosen arm reads that arm's pattern
    variable. Written as `@if` arms, the arm's identity would be the *type* — so moving the selection
    from one name to another name would not change the arm index, the region would survive, and every
    binding in it would go on showing the first name for ever. That is exactly
    `VariationHarnessView`'s bug, and the fix there was to put every readout back through a signal.
    Doing the same here means four snapshot records, one per arm, plus the five editing controls that
    write *back* into `selected` — which is a bigger change than this wave should make to a panel
    whose pane is genuinely a factory. `vocab-fields` is a `ref`'d host and `Restate` fills it, which
    is `SettingsView`'s pane pattern and `MaterialView`'s.

    ⚠ **The list is markup and its selection is a binding rather than a key, which is
    `TextureImportView`'s call and the opposite of `QueryView`'s.** A selected row is the same tag
    with one more class, and a class *can* be bound — so `selected` is not in the key: it reaches the
    screen through `ClassOf(row)`, which reads the row's own class and a `chosen` signal beside it. A
    key carrying the flag would work and would tear down and rebuild two rows on every press for
    nothing. ⚠ **And here it would do worse than nothing**: the hit test holds a list of
    `(key, entry)` pairs, and a key that changed when the selection moved would leave every pair in
    that list naming a row that no longer exists.

    ⚠ **The class order is load-bearing and is the hand-written one.** `Row` writes `member` then
    `missing`, and `Restate` writes `selected` after both — so `ClassOf` composes them in that order
    and a whole-tree dump prints `.member .missing .selected` exactly as it did. `SetClasses` splits
    on spaces and adds in order, which is what makes one string enough.

    ⚠ **A heading is a shorter row, and the flag is in the key.** `Heading` builds a `vocab-row` with
    a `vocab-name` and no `vocab-detail`; every other row has both. That is an `@if` inside the loop
    body, so the flag has to be in the key or a row that turned into a heading would keep its second
    cell — and unlike the selection, `Heading` is a fact about what the row *is* rather than about
    what is chosen, so it never changes without the rest of the record changing too.

    ⚠ **`refs` is here for a hit test and not for a handler**, which is the second use
    `VariationHarnessView` found. A `vocab-row` is a plain element and plain elements raise no click;
    the press is resolved by asking each row whether it contains the point, in the order the document
    declares them. `ElementRefs` refuses to enumerate, which is right: the row list is already the
    authority on that order, and offering a second one is offering one to get wrong.

    ⚠ **And the `@if` is *inside* the row rather than around it, which is why this file is worth
    reading if a `refs` ever misbehaves.** The obvious shape — a heading arm and a detail arm, each
    with its own `<vocab-row key refs class>` — is what was written first, and it compiled and drew
    seven empty boxes. `BuildContext.For` sets the iteration key around a *synchronous* build and
    `Switch` registers a deferred effect, so a `refs` in an arm found no key and threw, abandoning
    the arm's builder after the element and before its classes, bindings and children. Fixed in
    `BuildContext.Switch` the same day (2026-08-23) and asserted by
    `CompositionTests.A_refs_inside_a_branch_inside_a_row_is_filed_under_the_rows_key`, so both
    spellings work now — this one is kept because one row element with a conditional second cell is
    the better markup regardless, and because the heading arm was duplicating the whole row to change
    one child.

    ⚠ **The geometric hit test is kept, bug and all.** A press outside `vocab-list` but inside some
    row's rectangle selects that row, including a row scrolled out of view. Routing the press through
    `on:pointerdown` on each row would fix it for free and was the more natural markup; it was not
    done, for the reason wave 4 gave about the harness matrix — a port that changes behaviour is a
    defect until argued for, and this belongs in a commit that says so.
-->
@component ShapeVocabularyView
@namespace Vixen.Editor.AssetEditors.Animation
@inherits Vixen.Ui.Controls.Control
@tag vocab-editor
@using System.Collections.Immutable
@using Vixen.Animation.Constraints
@using Vixen.Core.Mathematics
@using Vixen.Ui
@using Vixen.Ui.Composition
@using Vixen.Ui.Controls
@using Vixen.Ui.Reactive

<vocab-bar ref="@Bar">
    <Button ref="@AddTerm" Label="Add Name" />
    <Button ref="@AddTag" Label="Add Tag" />
    <Button ref="@AddPlan" Label="Add Class" />
    <Button ref="@AddMember" Label="Add Required Shape" />
    <Button ref="@Drop" Label="Remove" />
</vocab-bar>

<vocab-body>
    <vocab-list ref="@List">
        @for (var row in Rows) {
            <vocab-row key="@row" refs="@RowElements" class="@ClassOf(row)">
                <VocabName Text="@row.Name" />

                @if (!row.Heading) {
                    <VocabDetail Text="@row.Detail" />
                }
            </vocab-row>
        }
    </vocab-list>

    <vocab-side>
        <vocab-fields ref="@Fields" />

        <vocab-report ref="@Report">
            @if (Problems.Length > 0) {
                <VocabTitle Text="Problems" />
            }

            @for (var problem in Problems) {
                <VocabNote key="@problem" class="@problem.Class" Text="@problem.Message" />
            }
        </vocab-report>
    </vocab-side>
</vocab-body>

@code {
    /// <summary>Every declared thing, in one list, headings included.</summary>
    readonly Signal<ImmutableArray<VocabularyRow>> listed = new([]);

    /// <summary>Which row is chosen, as the slot the loop keys carry, or −1.</summary>
    readonly Signal<int> chosen = new(-1);

    /// <summary>What is wrong with the file, as the build would say it.</summary>
    readonly Signal<ImmutableArray<VocabularyProblemRow>> problems = new([]);

    /// <summary>What each hit-testable row is, in the order the document declares them.</summary>
    readonly List<(VocabularyRow Key, object Entry)> hits = [];

    ShapeVocabularyDocument? document;
    object? selected;

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override bool AcceptsFocus => false;

    /// <summary>The bar across the top.</summary>
    public UiElement Bar { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>Declares a name a shape may have.</summary>
    public Button AddTerm { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>Declares a tag a shape may carry.</summary>
    public Button AddTag { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>Declares a body plan. Named <c>AddPlan</c> because <c>UiElement.AddClass</c> exists.</summary>
    public Button AddPlan { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>Adds a shape the selected class requires.</summary>
    public Button AddMember { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>Removes whatever is selected. Named <c>Drop</c> because <c>UiElement.Remove</c> exists.</summary>
    public Button Drop { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>Everything the file declares, in one list.</summary>
    public UiElement List { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>The selected row's fields.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>A host element <see cref="Restate" /> fills, and the file header carries the
    ///     argument.</b> The pane is a <c>switch</c> over the selection's <i>type</i>, and an
    ///     <c>@if</c> arm keyed on a type is wave 4's surviving-region trap at its sharpest: moving
    ///     from one name to another would not change the arm, so nothing in it would be rebuilt and
    ///     every field would go on showing the first name. An element owned by no region is safe to
    ///     clear and refill.
    /// </remarks>
    public UiElement Fields { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>What is wrong with the file, as the build would say it.</summary>
    public UiElement Report { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>Every row that can be pressed, by the key the loop reconciled it on.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>A hit test's handle rather than a handler's</b>, which is <c>refs</c>' second use. A
    ///     <c>vocab-row</c> is a plain element and raises no click; a list of rows is deliberately not
    ///     a list of controls.
    /// </remarks>
    internal ElementRefs<UiElement> RowElements { get; } = new();

    /// <summary>What is selected, or <see langword="null" />.</summary>
    public object? Selected => selected;

    partial void OnComposed() {
        AddHandler<ClickEvent>(static (element, args) => ((ShapeVocabularyView) element).Chosen(args));
        AddHandler<PointerEvent>(static (element, args) => ((ShapeVocabularyView) element).Pressed(args));
    }

    /// <summary>Shows a vocabulary.</summary>
    /// <param name="vocabulary">The document.</param>
    public void Show(ShapeVocabularyDocument vocabulary) {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(vocabulary);

        if (document is { } previous) {
            previous.Changed -= Reload;
        }

        document = vocabulary;
        vocabulary.Changed += Reload;

        Reload(vocabulary);
    }

    /// <summary>Rebuilds from the document.</summary>
    /// <param name="vocabulary">The document.</param>
    public void Reload(ShapeVocabularyDocument vocabulary) {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(vocabulary);
        Reload();
    }

    /// <summary>Rebuilds the list.</summary>
    public void Reload() {
        hits.Clear();

        if (document is not { } vocabulary) {
            listed.Value = [];

            Restate();
            return;
        }

        // The names a class member may refer to, so a member that refers to something else can be
        // marked as it is drawn rather than only when the check is run.
        var declared = new HashSet<string>(
            vocabulary.Vocabulary.Shapes.Select(static term => term.Name),
            StringComparer.Ordinal
        );

        var rows = ImmutableArray.CreateBuilder<VocabularyRow>();
        var slot = 0;

        rows.Add(Heading(slot++, "Names"));

        foreach (var term in vocabulary.Vocabulary.Shapes) {
            rows.Add(Row(slot++, term, term.Name, term.Meaning, false, 0));
        }

        rows.Add(Heading(slot++, "Tags"));

        foreach (var tag in vocabulary.Vocabulary.Tags) {
            rows.Add(Row(slot++, tag, tag.Tag, tag.Meaning, false, 0));
        }

        rows.Add(Heading(slot++, "Classes"));

        foreach (var declaredClass in vocabulary.Vocabulary.Classes) {
            rows.Add(
                Row(slot++, declaredClass, declaredClass.Name, $"{declaredClass.Members.Length} required shape(s)", false, 0)
            );

            foreach (var member in declaredClass.Members) {
                var missing = declared.Count > 0 && !declared.Contains(member.Name);

                rows.Add(
                    Row(
                        slot++,
                        new VocabularyMember(declaredClass, member),
                        member.Name,
                        missing ? "not declared above" : $"{member.Kind}{(member.Required ? string.Empty : ", optional")}",
                        missing,
                        1
                    )
                );
            }
        }

        listed.Value = rows.ToImmutable();

        Restate();
    }

    static VocabularyRow Heading(int slot, string text) => new(slot, true, "header", text, string.Empty);

    VocabularyRow Row(int slot, object entry, string name, string detail, bool missing, int depth) {
        // ⚠ Composed in the order the hand-written panel wrote them — `member`, then `missing`, and
        // `selected` after both, which `ClassOf` appends. `Tree()` prints classes in the order they
        // were added, so an order that read well and differed here would be a dump that differed.
        var classes = depth > 0
            ? missing ? "member missing" : "member"
            : missing ? "missing" : string.Empty;

        var row = new VocabularyRow(slot, false, classes, name, detail);

        hits.Add((row, entry));

        return row;
    }

    /// <summary>The classes a row wears, which is its own plus the selection.</summary>
    /// <param name="row">The row.</param>
    /// <returns>The class attribute's value.</returns>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>A binding rather than a key, which is what keeps the hit test's pairs valid.</b> The
    ///     selection is the one thing about a row that changes without the row changing; putting it
    ///     in the key would change the key, and <see cref="hits" /> holds the old one.
    /// </remarks>
    string ClassOf(VocabularyRow row) {
        if (row.Slot != chosen.Value) {
            return row.Class;
        }

        return row.Class.Length > 0 ? row.Class + " selected" : "selected";
    }

    void Restate() {
        Fields.Empty();

        var slot = -1;

        foreach (var (key, entry) in hits) {
            if (Same(entry, selected)) {
                slot = key.Slot;
                break;
            }
        }

        chosen.Value = slot;

        if (document is not { } vocabulary) {
            problems.Value = [];
            return;
        }

        Fields.Add("vocab-title").Text = vocabulary.Vocabulary.Name;

        if (vocabulary.LoadError is { } error) {
            Fields.Add("vocab-error").Text = error;
        }

        Fields.Add("fact-row").Add("fact-name").Text =
            $"{vocabulary.Vocabulary.Shapes.Length} name(s), {vocabulary.Vocabulary.Tags.Length} tag(s), "
            + $"{vocabulary.Vocabulary.Classes.Length} class(es)";

        switch (selected) {
            case ShapeTermRecord term:
                Term(vocabulary, term);
                break;

            case ShapeTagRecord tag:
                Afford(vocabulary, tag);
                break;

            case ShapeClassRecord declared:
                Class(vocabulary, declared);
                break;

            case VocabularyMember member:
                Member(vocabulary, member);
                break;

            default:
                Fields.Add("text").Text = "Select a name, a tag or a class.";
                break;
        }

        // ⚠ Always shown, not behind a button. The one mistake this file exists to prevent is one an
        // author makes while typing a class, and a check they have to remember to press is one they
        // press after they have finished.
        var found = vocabulary.Problems();
        var notes = ImmutableArray.CreateBuilder<VocabularyProblemRow>(found.Count);
        var line = 0;

        foreach (var problem in found) {
            notes.Add(new(line++, problem.Fatal ? "fatal" : string.Empty, problem.Message));
        }

        problems.Value = notes.MoveToImmutable();
    }

    void Term(ShapeVocabularyDocument vocabulary, ShapeTermRecord term) {
        Fields.Add("vocab-title").Text = "Name";

        Line("Name", term.Name, value => selected = vocabulary.Edit(term, entry => entry with { Name = value }));

        // ⚠ The meaning is the useful half and the panel says so by giving it the same standing as
        // the name. A vocabulary of thirty bare words is a list nobody can use to settle an argument.
        Line("Meaning", term.Meaning, value => selected = vocabulary.Edit(term, entry => entry with { Meaning = value }));
    }

    void Afford(ShapeVocabularyDocument vocabulary, ShapeTagRecord tag) {
        Fields.Add("vocab-title").Text = "Tag";

        Line("Tag", tag.Tag, value => selected = vocabulary.Edit(tag, entry => entry with { Tag = value }));
        Line("Meaning", tag.Meaning, value => selected = vocabulary.Edit(tag, entry => entry with { Meaning = value }));
    }

    void Class(ShapeVocabularyDocument vocabulary, ShapeClassRecord declared) {
        Fields.Add("vocab-title").Text = "Class";

        Line("Name", declared.Name, value => selected = vocabulary.Edit(declared, entry => entry with { Name = value }));

        Fields.Add("text").Text =
            "A body that claims this class has to carry every shape below it. That is what makes one authored "
            + "contact portable: a clip may assume the names exist.";
    }

    void Member(ShapeVocabularyDocument vocabulary, VocabularyMember member) {
        Fields.Add("vocab-title").Text = "Required shape";

        Line(
            "Name",
            member.Member.Name,
            value => selected = vocabulary.Edit(member, entry => entry with { Name = value })
        );

        var kind = Fields.Add("fact-row");
        kind.Add("fact-name").Text = "Kind";

        var select = kind.Add("fact-value").Add<Select>();

        foreach (var option in Enum.GetValues<ShapeKind>()) {
            select.AddOption(option.ToString());
        }

        select.Value = member.Member.Kind.ToString();

        select.SelectionChanged += (_, value) => {
            if (Enum.TryParse<ShapeKind>(value, out var picked)) {
                selected = vocabulary.Edit(member, entry => entry with { Kind = picked });
            }
        };

        Vector(
            "Extents",
            member.Member.Extents,
            value => selected = vocabulary.Edit(member, entry => entry with { Extents = value })
        );

        var required = Fields.Add("fact-row");
        required.Add("fact-name").Text = "Required";

        var toggle = required.Add("fact-value").Add<ToggleButton>();

        toggle.Label = member.Member.Required ? "a set without it is invalid" : "optional";
        toggle.IsChecked = member.Member.Required;

        toggle.CheckedChanged += (_, on) => selected = vocabulary.Edit(member, entry => entry with { Required = on });
    }

    void Line(string label, string value, Action<string> write) {
        var row = Fields.Add("fact-row");

        row.Add("fact-name").Text = label;

        var box = row.Add("fact-value").Add<TextBox>();

        box.Value = value;
        box.ValueChanged += (_, text) => write(text ?? string.Empty);
    }

    void Vector(string label, Vector3 value, Action<Vector3> write) {
        var row = Fields.Add("fact-row");

        row.Add("fact-name").Text = label;

        var holder = row.Add("fact-value");
        var current = value;

        Component(holder, current.X, number => write(new(number, current.Y, current.Z)));
        Component(holder, current.Y, number => write(new(current.X, number, current.Z)));
        Component(holder, current.Z, number => write(new(current.X, current.Y, number)));
    }

    static void Component(UiElement holder, float value, Action<float> write) {
        var box = holder.Add<NumericInput>();

        box.Decimals = 3;
        box.Number = value;
        box.NumberChanged += (_, number) => write((float) number);
    }

    /// <summary>
    ///     ⚠ <b>A member is compared by its class and its record, not by reference to the wrapper.</b>
    ///     Every rebuild makes a fresh <see cref="VocabularyMember" /> around the same two objects, so
    ///     reference equality on the wrapper would lose the selection on every keystroke.
    /// </summary>
    static bool Same(object? left, object? right) =>
        left is VocabularyMember first && right is VocabularyMember second
            ? ReferenceEquals(first.Member, second.Member)
            : ReferenceEquals(left, right);

    void Chosen(ClickEvent args) {
        if (document is not { } vocabulary) {
            return;
        }

        for (var element = args.Source; element is not null; element = element.Parent) {
            if (ReferenceEquals(element, AddTerm)) {
                selected = vocabulary.AddTerm();
                args.Handled = true;

                return;
            }

            if (ReferenceEquals(element, AddTag)) {
                selected = vocabulary.AddTag();
                args.Handled = true;

                return;
            }

            if (ReferenceEquals(element, AddPlan)) {
                selected = vocabulary.AddClass();
                args.Handled = true;

                return;
            }

            if (ReferenceEquals(element, AddMember)) {
                // A member belongs to a class, so the button follows the selection into one rather
                // than asking which — and does nothing when the selection is not in a class at all.
                if (Owner() is { } owner) {
                    selected = vocabulary.AddMember(owner);
                }

                args.Handled = true;
                return;
            }

            if (ReferenceEquals(element, Drop)) {
                Delete(vocabulary);
                args.Handled = true;

                return;
            }
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Which class the Add Required Shape button would add to, or <see langword="null" />.</summary>
    public ShapeClassRecord? Owner() =>
        selected switch {
            ShapeClassRecord declared => declared,
            VocabularyMember member => member.Class,
            _ => null
        };

    void Delete(ShapeVocabularyDocument vocabulary) {
        var removed = selected switch {
            ShapeTermRecord term => vocabulary.Remove(term),
            ShapeTagRecord tag => vocabulary.Remove(tag),
            ShapeClassRecord declared => vocabulary.Remove(declared),
            VocabularyMember member => vocabulary.Remove(member),
            _ => false
        };

        if (removed) {
            selected = null;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    ///     ⚠ A row is a plain element and plain elements raise no click, so a press is turned into a
    ///     selection by asking each row whether it contains the point.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b><see cref="hits" />' order, because <c>ElementRefs</c> refuses to enumerate.</b> That
    ///     refusal is right: the row list is already the authority on what order the rows are in, and
    ///     a second answer is one to get wrong.
    /// </remarks>
    void Pressed(PointerEvent args) {
        if (args.Action != PointerAction.Pressed || args.Button != PointerButton.Primary) {
            return;
        }

        foreach (var (key, entry) in hits) {
            if (RowElements.TryGet(key, out var element) && element.Bounds.Contains(new Vector2(args.X, args.Y))) {
                selected = entry;
                Restate();

                args.Handled = true;
                return;
            }
        }
    }

    // ================================================================== What the markup reads

    ImmutableArray<VocabularyRow> Rows => listed.Value;

    ImmutableArray<VocabularyProblemRow> Problems => problems.Value;
}
